Predicting CO₂ Emissions Using Total Energy Supply and Socioeconomic Indicators

A comparative analysis of regression models 

Problem statement

Environmental analysts need to understand how energy supply, population
and economic activity relate to national emissions. These relationships
may vary across countries and years.

This project will investigate how effectively regression models estimate
annual country-level CO₂ emissions from these indicators. Predictive
associations will not be interpreted as causal effects.

Proposed approach

Use country-year observations to compare three regression algorithms.

Initial predictors:
- Total energy supply
- Population
- Gross domestic product

The algorithms and final study period will be documented before modelling.
Regional and world aggregates will be excluded. Countries, territories and
historical entities will be reviewed to establish a consistent geographic scope.

The study estimates emissions using indicators for the same year.
It does not initially forecast future emissions from earlier information.

 Dataset

Source: Our World in Data, CO₂ and greenhouse gas emissions dataset.
Documentation: https://catalog.ourworldindata.org/emissions/owid_co2/
The catalogue reports an update date of 10 November 2026.

Initial inspection plan
1. Check dataset structure, types and identifiers.
2. Review geographic entities and distinguish aggregates.
3. Inspect missingness by variable, country and year.
4. Check duplicate country-year observations.
5. Examine variable ranges, zeros and suspicious values.
6. Compare candidate study periods using predictor coverage.
7. Identify variables that could leak information about the target.

Exploratory inspection helps reveal structure and anomalies before
modelling assumptions are made (NIST/SEMATECH, n.d.).

 Planned modelling safeguards

- Preserve the original downloaded file.
- Document each cleaning decision and its effect on sample size.
- Do not use emissions-derived indicators as initial predictors.
- Fit learned preprocessing using training data only.
- Select validation methods that respect repeated countries and years.
- Evaluate models using MAE, RMSE and R².
- Reserve a final test set from model selection.

These safeguards address leakage and dependence between observations
(Kaufman, Rosset and Perlich, 2011; scikit-learn developers, n.d.a; n.d.b).

Limitations to investigate

- Unequal country and year coverage.
- Missing GDP and energy observations.
- Changes in national boundaries and reporting.
- Differences in country size.
- Large emissions values that may be valid observations.
- Predictor availability when the model would be used.
- Predictive feature importance does not establish causation.

Reuse and attribution

OWID combines multiple original sources. Their original licensing
conditions still apply. Source attribution and applicable terms will be
recorded before distributing data.

 Project structure

data/raw/          Original downloaded data
data/processed/    Data produced by documented cleaning
notebooks/         Inspection, cleaning and modelling notebooks
src/               Reusable code
reports/figures/   Figures for the assessment
